\subsection{The overlap} \label{S9}
An overlap of $B_{1}$ is a column $I=(M_I, \epsilon)\in d (\mathbf{y} ,w)$ that matches at least two different jobs from $B_{1}$ with machines in $\mathcal{G}_{1}$. Similarly, an overlap of $B_{2}$ is a column $I=(M_I, \epsilon) \in d (\mathbf{y} ,w)$ that matches at least two different jobs from $B_{2}$ with machines in $\mathcal{G}_{2}$. 


\begin{lemma}
\label{overlap} An overlap of $B_{1}$ and an overlap of $B_{2}$  do not occur simultaneously. 
\end{lemma}


\begin{proof}
Suppose for contradiction that both overlaps occur simultaneously. Then there are different jobs $a$ and $g$ both from $B_{1}$ done on $\mathcal{G}_{1}$ in a column $I_{a ,g} \in d (\mathbf{y} ,w)$ of type $\binom{ \ast  ,a ,g}{ \ast }$, and different jobs $b$ and $k$ both from $B_{2}$ done on $\mathcal{G}_{2}$ in a column $I_{b ,k} \in d (\mathbf{y} ,w)$ of type $\binom{ \ast }{ \ast  ,b ,k}$. By Lemma \ref{b1b2}, $I_{a ,g}$ is of type $\binom{ \ast  ,a ,b ,k ,g}{ \ast }$ and $I_{b ,k}$ is of type $\binom{ \ast }{ \ast  ,a ,b ,k ,g}$. This, by Theorem \ref{eps}, contradicts Lemma
\ref{abgkpath}  (by Theorem \ref{T4} there are no crossing jobs thus all four jobs $a$, $g$, $b$, and $k$ are different). This proves the lemma.
\end{proof}

