\section{Integral optimal solution to $\ell p$ for $\sum_{j\in B_1} \varepsilon_j=\epsilon$ or $ \sum_{j\in B_2} \varepsilon_j=\epsilon$.} \label{S10}

In this section we prove that an integral optimal solution for $\ell p$ exists if $\epsilon>0$ and  $\sum_{j\in B_1} \varepsilon_j=\epsilon$ or $ \sum_{j\in B_2} \varepsilon_j=\epsilon$. We first prove this assuming $\sum_{j\in B_2} \varepsilon_j=\epsilon$ in $\mathbf{s}$ throughout this section. The proof for  $\sum_{j\in B_1} \varepsilon_j=\epsilon$  proceeds in a similar fashion and thus will be omitted. 
\end{proof}




